1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,514
It uses the old policy

2
00:00:02,814 --> 00:00:05,631
and picks a side when documents disagree.

3
00:00:06,967 --> 00:00:08,461
Two classic failures.

4
00:00:08,761 --> 00:00:13,156
A runbook from twenty twenty-four still ranks high, because it is relevant.

5
00:00:13,456 --> 00:00:18,342
And when a newer memo contradicts the handbook, most pipelines quietly pick one.

6
00:00:18,642 --> 00:00:22,504
Neither failure looks like an error. Both sound confident.

7
00:00:23,867 --> 00:00:29,499
In a PackSpec, freshness is part of the requirement. Here, nothing older than a year.

8
00:00:29,799 --> 00:00:36,072
On its own, the twenty twenty-four runbook is rejected as stale, so the check is inconclusive.

9
00:00:36,372 --> 00:00:44,629
Add the current handbook and a memo that contradicts it, and the check refuses. It doesn't pick a side. It shows the contradiction.

10
00:00:45,967 --> 00:00:49,402
Age and contradiction are explicit in the result.

11
00:00:49,702 --> 00:01:00,945
But QualityRag only knows what your metadata says: when a passage was observed, and whether it contradicts. Spotting supersession in raw text is still on the roadmap.

12
00:01:01,245 --> 00:01:05,363
Next, a research preview: tables your parser quietly broke.
